---
permalink: /sitemap.xml
eleventyExcludeFromCollections: true
---
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<urlset xmlns="http://www.sitemaps.org/schemas/sitemap/0.9">
  {%- for page in collections.all %}
  {%- if not page.data.eleventyExcludeFromCollections %}
  <url>
    <loc>https://transpersonal-training.com{{ page.url }}</loc>
    {#-
      No <lastmod> on purpose — don't add one back without reading this.

      It used to emit `page.date`, which in CI is the checkout time, so every
      page claimed to have changed on every single deploy. Google ignores a
      lastmod it finds untrustworthy, and a file that cries wolf on all 21 URLs
      is exactly how it earns that. No claim beats a false one.

      The obvious repair — the file's last git commit — is wrong here too:
      /schedule/, /lectures-schedule/, /curriculum/ and
      /become-a-practice-client/ are built from Google Sheets, so their content
      changes with no commit behind it. Git dates would understate precisely the
      pages where freshness actually matters.

      Doing this properly means pairing git dates for repo-backed pages with the
      real sheet timestamps (src/_data/sheetTimestamps.js already fetches them,
      though it doesn't expose them to templates yet). That's a fair amount of
      machinery for one optional field on a 21-page site — worth it only if
      something concrete comes to depend on the signal.
    -#}
    {%- if page.url == "/" %}
    <changefreq>weekly</changefreq>
    <priority>1.0</priority>
    {%- elif page.url | startsWith("/blog/") %}
    <changefreq>monthly</changefreq>
    <priority>0.7</priority>
    {%- else %}
    <changefreq>monthly</changefreq>
    <priority>0.8</priority>
    {%- endif %}
  </url>
  {%- endif %}
  {%- endfor %}
</urlset>
